#--: Units

#ul:
  [#*:
    modules are files and packages are directories
    #ul:
      [#*: one file, one module.  Cannot spread a module over multiple files, no
        include
        #ul:
          [#*: but can make a module that exports submodules, so you can control
            structure and expose it with a single exported var]
          [#*:
            can have a file (with extension) and a directory with the same name but:
            #ul:
              [#*: the file is the one that is loaded from 'outside', ie. the public
                part]
              [#*: the dir is only accessable from the file.  It contains private
                modules so the module can be split up into files but the top level
                file controls what is exposed to the outside world]
              [#*: submodules are searched in the dir first before looking in the global
                file tree]
            ]
          ]
        ]
  [#*: packages (dirs) can only contain modules (files)]
  [#*: a name written in a module resolves outward: the module itself, then each
    module enclosing it up to the file unit, then core. A submodule sees its
    parents; the restriction is the other way (see privacy below)]
  [#*: modules (and packages) 'own' the namespace where they are located]
  [#*: modules can shadow vars in other files and dirs (but only within themselves)
    #ul:
      [#*: to find a var definition, look in current module, if not found, go look
      in the directory tree (first relative, then from the top level module path)]
    ]
  [#*: a unit's top-level definitions are all public by default. A
    'public: unit { ... }' block restricts the unit's public interface to the
    listed definitions; an unlisted definition is private and unreachable from
    another unit (E0101). An entry may rename what it exposes -- ':name' exposes
    'name' as itself, 'alias: internal' exposes 'internal' under the name 'alias'
    (see the Visibility section of the language spec).]
  [#*: a 'require:' block states what the unit needs from the host toolchain:
    'require: unit { library: "quadmath" }'. It is an ordinary inline unit, so
    it is written and parsed like any other; what is layered over it is a
    schema. Every entry must be a key the compiler consumes -- today only
    'library:', whose value is a plain string literal naming a link library --
    and the block must declare at least one requirement, because a key nothing
    reads would be silently ignored (error E0038). The requirement applies only
    when the declaring unit is actually reached: a program that never uses the
    unit does not link its library.]
  [#*:
    modules and packages are never capitalised (to cope with case insensitive
    filesystems). So they are always public (but see private submodules above)
    #ul:
      [#*: there are other restrictions on module and package names because they
        must be valid identifiers (up to the extension) eg. can't start with a
        number]
      ]
  [#*:
    modules have no initialisation
    #ul:
      [#*: so they don't need to be imported]
      [#*: can't have any special data structures (just static, readonly simple vars
        and arrays (and dicts, but special simplified dict that are arrays inside
          and are not O(1) on access ?....)]
      [#*: any special state must be provided by an api.  Eg. an init function or
        similar]
    ]


#--: Runtime-backed stdlib units

The core stdlib units (#~"system", #~"core", #~"collections",
#~"io", #~"os", #~"cli") are declaration surfaces for the C runtime:
their types and #~"is native" functions are implemented under
#~"src/runtime", while bodied free functions in them are ordinary
compiled zerolang (demand-gated). Type methods there cannot carry
#~".z" bodies. Details in the compiler doc's "Runtime-backed stdlib
units" section.

#--: Implementation status

The parser (#~"lib/system/zparser.z" -- #~"loadUnit" / #~"parse") implements the search
path above: #~"core" is loaded first, and a referenced name's submodules in a
same-named directory are searched before the top-level tree. The type checker's
bare-name resolution mirrors the same precedence (current unit first, then the
stdlib units) and locks each definition to one canonical type id -- see
#~"typechecker.pdoc:Type-name resolution and the canonical-id rule". Real
programs (the test corpus and the self-hosted compiler) use flat units with
#_*"dotted" cross-unit references (#~"zast.Node"), but the
same-named-directory submodule form is typechecked, compiled and run: see
#~"tests/fixtures/emitc_corpus/hidden_subunit.z" and #~"subunit_depth.z", a
subunit with a subunit of its own.

Two visibility rules follow from how each form is reached. An
#_*"inline unit" is an ordinary top-level definition and so is public by
default, subject to its unit's #~"public:" block. An
#_*"implementation subunit" is private to the unit that owns it by default:
its file is read only because that unit named it, so it is not on the default
surface, and reaching past a unit into one is an error (E0101). A
#~"public:" block is the one visibility mechanism: when the unit has one, the
block is the surface, and a subunit listed there (#~"public: unit { :impl }")
is public -- the listing is what loads it
(#~"tests/fixtures/emitc_corpus/public_subunit_listed.z"). Privacy governs who may
reference #_*"into" a subunit; it says nothing about what the subunit sees. A
subunit resolves outward like every other unit: its own definitions, then its
parent's, up through the file unit, then #~"core"
(#~"tests/fixtures/emitc_corpus/subunit_parent_read.z" and
#~"subunit_grandparent_read.z").

